# Build context for deploy/Dockerfile. DENY EVERYTHING, then allow five files.
#
# WHY THIS IS AN ALLOWLIST
#
# `fly launch` auto-detected this repo as a generic Python app and generated a
# root Dockerfile containing `COPY . .` with a five-line .dockerignore. That
# would have shipped 5.0 GB to a public host: the gold set, the adjudication
# queue, every eval run, the corpora, 3.8 GB of trained adapters, and
# `scripts/webui.py` — whose script runner executes training and evaluation on
# the host and is remote code execution on a public URL. Nothing was deployed,
# but a generated GitHub workflow would have pushed it on the next commit to
# main.
#
# The image built from deploy/Dockerfile only ever contained four files, because
# its COPY list is explicit. **The build context is a separate exposure**: fly
# uploads the whole context to its remote builder before the COPY runs, so
# without this file the gold set and the adapters are transmitted regardless of
# what ends up in the image.
#
# So this denies by default and names what may leave the machine, which is the
# same discipline `webui.py`'s ACTIONS allowlist uses and for the same reason: a
# blocklist ships anything added later, and the thing added later is exactly the
# thing nobody thought about.
#
# HOW THE PATTERNS WORK
#
# Docker resolves each path by LAST MATCHING PATTERN. A bare `*` followed by
# `!some/nested/file` relies on the builder choosing to descend into a directory
# it has already excluded — real behaviour, but version-dependent and easy to
# get subtly wrong. So every parent directory is re-included explicitly, then
# its contents re-excluded, then the one wanted file re-included. Verbose, and
# it depends on nothing but last-match-wins.
#
# ADDING A LINE HERE MEANS SOMETHING NEW IS LEAVING THE MACHINE. That is the
# moment to check it against the boundary in deploy/Dockerfile.

# --- deny everything -------------------------------------------------------
*

# --- scripts/: the server and its single project import --------------------
!scripts
scripts/*
!scripts/rubric_server.py
!scripts/common.py

# --- data/: ONLY the exported task file -----------------------------------
# Not gold_questions.jsonl, not positions.jsonl, not the adjudication queue,
# not judge_adjudications.jsonl. The task file is self-contained by
# construction (`--export-tasks`) precisely so none of those need to travel.
!data
data/*
!data/gold
data/gold/*
!data/gold/worksheets
data/gold/worksheets/*
!data/gold/worksheets/tasks.json
!data/gold/worksheets/rubric_tasks.json
!data/gold/worksheets/position_tasks.json

# --- deploy/: the three-line requirements ----------------------------------
!deploy
deploy/*
!deploy/requirements.txt

# --- never, under any pattern above ----------------------------------------
# Listed last so they win even if a future `!` line above is written too
# broadly. Belt and braces: the cost of a redundant line here is zero, and the
# cost of a missing one is the gold set on a public builder.
.git
.env
.envrc
**/__pycache__
mlx_env
models
